course: biology
lesson: 271
title: The asteroid: the day the dinosaurs died and who survived
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: About 66 million years ago an asteroid about 10 kilometres wide, as wide as a big city, hit what is now Mexico at about 20 kilometres a second and left the Chicxulub crater, about 180 kilometres across.
explain: The blast threw hot rock into the sky that fell back and set off fires, made giant waves, and filled the air with dust and soot that darkened the sky for months or years.
explain: Without sunlight the plants could not make food, so they died, then the plant-eaters starved, then the meat-eaters: the food chain fell down from the bottom like a tower of blocks.
explain: Small animals that could burrow, hide in water, or eat seeds and dead things, such as frogs, turtles, crocodiles, small mammals and some birds, came through it.
explain: With the big dinosaurs gone, mammals spread and grew to every size, and our own ancestors were among them, so in a way we owe our lives to that rock from space.
fact: An asteroid about 10 kilometres wide hit Chicxulub in Mexico 66 million years ago at about 20 kilometres a second; the crater, about 180 kilometres wide, is now buried under land and sea.
fact: The blast was billions of times stronger than the biggest bomb ever made; it started fires, made giant waves and darkened the sky for months or years.
fact: All over the world a thin clay layer rich in iridium marks the end of the dinosaurs; iridium is rare on Earth but common in asteroids, a clue found by Luis and Walter Alvarez, father and son, in 1980.
fact: At Tanis in North Dakota, scientists report fish that died with tiny glass beads from the impact in their gills, which may record the very day; this find is still being studied.
fact: Without sunlight plants could not make food, so plant-eaters starved, and then meat-eaters: the food chain collapsed from the bottom.
fact: The Deccan Traps volcanoes in India poured out lava for hundreds of thousands of years at the same time; scientists debate how much they added, but most think the asteroid was the main killer.
fact: About three quarters of all kinds of life died out; frogs, turtles, crocodiles, small mammals and some birds survived.
fact: With the big dinosaurs gone, mammals spread and grew big, and within a few million years there were mammals of every size, our ancestors among them.
fact: Scientists track big asteroids, and none of the known large ones will hit Earth in the next hundred years.
fact: In 2022 the DART spacecraft bumped into an asteroid and changed its path, a test of how to protect Earth.
fact: Shooting stars are tiny bits of space dust, usually smaller than a grain of sand, burning up high in the air; many tonnes of dust fall on Earth every day.
fact: There have been five great mass extinctions; the end of the dinosaurs was the most recent, and the biggest, 252 million years ago, killed about nine in ten sea species.
exercise: Where did the asteroid hit? | Chicxulub, in Mexico | In the middle of Australia | At the North Pole | In the Sahara
exercise: About how wide was the asteroid? | About 10 kilometres | About 10 metres | About 1,000 kilometres | About as wide as a football pitch
exercise: About how wide is the Chicxulub crater? | About 180 kilometres | About 1 kilometre | About 18 metres | About 18,000 kilometres
exercise: Why did the sky go dark after the impact? | Dust and soot filled the air | The Sun went out | The Moon moved in front of the Sun | Clouds of water vapour froze
exercise: What marks the end of the dinosaurs in rocks all over the world? | A thin clay layer rich in iridium | A thick shiny layer of real gold | A long line of dinosaur footprints | A layer of fresh white snow
exercise: Why is iridium a clue to an asteroid? | It is rare on Earth but common in asteroids | It glows green in the dark when it falls from space | It is made inside the bodies of dinosaurs | It only ever comes out of erupting volcanoes
exercise: Who found the iridium clue in 1980? | Luis and Walter Alvarez, father and son | Mary Anning, digging on the Dorset coast | Charles Darwin, on his voyage round the world | Karl Landsteiner, the doctor of blood groups
exercise: What do the fish at Tanis have in their gills? | Tiny glass beads from the impact | Specks of shiny gold dust | Broken bits of dinosaur teeth | Little crystals of ice from the sky
exercise: Why did the plants die? | Without sunlight they could not make food | They were all eaten up by hungry dinosaurs | It rained far too much and they drowned | The soil turned to sugar and burned them
exercise: What happened after the plants died? | Plant-eaters starved, then meat-eaters | Meat-eaters grew bigger and stronger | Nothing else changed for the animals | Plant-eaters learned to eat rocks instead
exercise: What were the Deccan Traps? | Huge volcanoes in India pouring out lava | Traps for big animals set by early people | A long row of high mountains in Mexico | A kind of dinosaur nest made of mud and sticks
exercise: What do most scientists think was the main killer? | The asteroid | The volcanoes alone | A disease | Mammals eating dinosaur eggs
exercise: About how much of all kinds of life died out? | About three quarters | About one in a hundred | Every single kind | About half of one percent
exercise: Which of these survived? | Small mammals, frogs, turtles and crocodiles | Only T. rex and a few of its closest cousins | Only the giant long-necked sauropods | Nothing at all, not one single living thing
exercise: What happened to mammals after the dinosaurs died out? | They spread and grew to every size | They all died out too, soon after | They stayed tiny for ever and ever | They slowly turned into the first birds
exercise: What did the DART spacecraft do in 2022? | It bumped an asteroid and changed its path | It landed the first people safely on Mars | It blew up a dangerous asteroid near Earth | It caught an asteroid and brought it home
exercise: How many great mass extinctions have there been? | Five | One | Fifty | Two
exercise: What is a shooting star? | A tiny bit of space dust burning up in the air | A real star falling down to Earth from the sky | A plane with bright lights flying very high | A piece that has broken off the Moon
test: «big rock hits Mexico, how does that kill a T. rex in Canada lol» What is the best answer? | Dust and soot darkened the sky, so plants died and food chains collapsed | The rock bounced right across America and landed on it in Canada | The rock was as wide as the whole Earth, so it hit everything | It didn't, the T. rex in Canada survived and lived on for ages
test: What would happen to a food chain if the plants at the bottom died? | The animals that eat plants would starve, then the meat-eaters | Only the plants would be missing, and all the rest would be fine | The meat-eaters would get more food for ever and grow fatter | The animals would start making their own food from sunlight
test: «a shooting star!! is it gonna hit us and kill us like the dinos» What is right? | No, it is a speck of dust burning up high in the air | Yes, every shooting star hits the ground somewhere near | Yes, that is just how the dinosaurs saw theirs coming | No, it is a star moving house to another part of the sky
test: «so we'd not be here without that asteroid, that's mad» Is there truth in it? | Yes, mammals spread after the big dinosaurs were gone, our ancestors among them | No, people were already living alongside T. rex and they just carried on | No, humans came straight from dinosaurs, so we'd be here anyway | Yes, the asteroid carried the first people to Earth from space
test: Count them: after the disaster, a pond has 3 frogs, 2 turtles and 1 crocodile. How many legs are there in all? | 24 | 12 | 18 | 6
test: In a cartoon the dinosaurs look up, see the asteroid, and all fall over dead at once. What is closer to the truth? | Many died in fires and waves, and many more starved slowly in the dark months after | All dinosaurs everywhere died in the very same second, wherever they were standing | None died at first; they all saw it coming and walked to safer lands far away | The asteroid missed the Earth, and only the volcanoes in India mattered in the end
test: «are we gonna get smashed by an asteroid next year??» What is best? | Scientists track big ones, and no known large one will hit for a hundred years | Yes, a huge one is already coming next week and nobody can do anything | Nobody watches the sky at all, so we would never know until it hit | Asteroids never come anywhere near Earth, so there is nothing to fear
test: Why were small seed-eaters and burrowers more likely to survive? | They could hide and live on seeds and dead things while plants were gone | They were much stronger and tougher than the big dinosaurs ever were | They could fly away to another planet until the dust had cleared | They could not catch any illness, so nothing at all could harm them
test: A scientist finds the same thin clay layer in Italy and in New Zealand. What does it tell her? | The disaster reached the whole world at the same time | Two small asteroids hit those two places and nowhere else | The clay was carried there by dinosaurs on their feet | Clay always forms in Italy and floats over to New Zealand
test: «it was the volcanoes in India, not some asteroid» What is the fairest answer? | The volcanoes may have helped, but most scientists think the asteroid was the main killer | Yes, it was only the volcanoes; the asteroid story was made up for films | No, there were never any volcanoes in India at all, so it must be the asteroid | Both are made up; the dinosaurs simply got old and tired and died
test: Why does DART matter, even though it bumped a harmless asteroid? | It tested how to push a dangerous asteroid off course | It brought back a big lump of gold from the asteroid | It destroyed all the asteroids that could ever hit us | It took photos of dinosaur bones lying on the asteroid
exam: Identify the location of the impact which is understood to have caused the extinction of the non-bird dinosaurs. | The Chicxulub site in Mexico | The interior of Australia | The North Pole | The Sahara desert
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the significance of iridium in the boundary clay. | Its abundance indicates an extraterrestrial origin, the element being rare on Earth but common in asteroids | It demonstrates that the dinosaurs perished by a disease which left this metal within their bones and the soil | It was deposited by the dinosaurs themselves over many millions of years before their final extinction | It is a substance found exclusively within volcanoes and therefore proves a volcanic cause of extinction
exam: Ascertain the principal mechanism by which the darkening of the sky brought about the collapse of food chains. | The cessation of plant growth for want of sunlight, followed by the starvation of herbivores and then carnivores | The direct poisoning of all animals by the darkness itself, which was understood to be toxic to reptiles | The freezing solid of all the oceans within a single day, which prevented the animals from drinking | The flight of all animals to other continents where the sky had remained clear and plants still grew
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the consequence of the extinction for the mammals? | They diversified and attained a great range of sizes within a few million years | They perished in their entirety alongside the dinosaurs, leaving no descendants whatever | They were reduced permanently to a minute size and never again exceeded that of a mouse | They became the ancestors of the birds by developing feathers
